Post Date: 2019-07-02T06:15:04Z
Current Date: April 4 2025
Title: OPTIONS request doesn't follow the order of route declaration
Description: I just notice that an OPTIONS request doesn't follow the order of route declaration and applies for all the routes that are defined using the same instance. 
I am not sure if it's bug or an intended behaviour since I don't fully understand the nature of OPTIONS request.

Here are 2 code snippets

```js
const app = require('express')();

app.use((req, res, next) => {
  res.send('Blocked');
});

app.get('/route1', (req, res) => {
  res.send('route1');
});

app.get('/route2', (req, res) => {
  res.send('route2')
});

app.listen(3000, () => {
  console.log('Server started on port: 3000');
});
```

```js
const app = require('express')();

app.get('/route1', (req, res) => {
  res.send('route1');
});

app.use((req, res, next) => {
  res.send('Blocked');
});

app.get('/route2', (req, res) => {
  res.send('route2')
});

app.listen(3000, () => {
  console.log('Server started on port: 3000');
});
```

In first case, both GET and OPTIONS request to any of the 2 routes returns the message "blocked" as expected since the middleware is defined prior to both and hence any request to either of these will first pass through it.

In second case, both GET and OPTIONS request to the second route return the message "blocked". Again as expected.  But for the first route, the GET request return the message "route1" while the OPTIONS request returns the message "Blocked". 

Why did the GET request was successfully served but the OPTIONS request first passed through the middleware that was defined after that route?

I'm using v4.17.1

